**DMU and TSP Analysis — Findings**

In response to the request to evaluate whether changes are needed across all three queues (DIR, DMU, TSP):

**1. Do we need to make changes in DMU or TSP?**
No. DMU and TSP determine eligibility through entirely different mechanisms that do not rely on the ELD0/ELDH/ELDX calendar dates:
- DMU eligibility is based on an existing delinquency indicator already present on the loan record.
- TSP eligibility is based on separate tax-sale reporting data.

Neither queue uses the 20-day calendar rule this story is correcting, so the specific reliability issue described in this story does not apply to them.

One item worth confirming: TSP does reference the calendar dates for a secondary due-date display, through separate logic that was recently updated (independently of this story) to use the **latest** available date. This story is updating DIR's equivalent logic to use the **earliest** available date instead. We recommend confirming this difference is intentional, since the two queues will end up using different rules against the same underlying calendar data.

**2. Would our changes impact DMU or TSP's existing records?**
Potentially, if not handled carefully. The stored procedure responsible for creating and updating these records is shared across all three queues. To prevent any impact, the changes being made for DIR will be explicitly scoped to apply only when processing DIR records, leaving DMU and TSP logic untouched. This will be verified through testing after implementation.

**Recommendation:** No functional changes are required for DMU or TSP as part of this story. Implementation will be scoped specifically to DIR, with verification testing to confirm DMU and TSP continue to behave exactly as they do today.

